## F. Tests

- [ ] <UNIT_TEST_COMMAND> - green, in this session, with the count recorded.
- [ ] <INTEGRATION_OR_SYSTEM_TEST_COMMAND> - green, or explicitly N/A with the
      reason.
- [ ] New tests cover the failure branch, not only the success branch.
- [ ] Test count before and after recorded in the report.

---

## G. Context-document budget

- [ ] If this change extended a document listed in `BUDGETED_DOCS.md`, its
      budget check ran and the **measured** number is recorded in the report -
      not an estimate, not "should be fine".
- [ ] Headroom is above the warning threshold. If it is not, say so; do not let
      the next unit discover it.
      *(A shared reviewer-prompt budget in the reference deployment sat 53
      characters from a hard failure while three parked units each added rules
      to counted sections.)*

---

## H. Documentation

- [ ] Any owner-visible behaviour change updated its documentation **in the
      same change**. A design document describing the old behaviour is worse
      than none.
- [ ] A new document has a row in the `AGENTS.md` doc index.
- [ ] The project's consolidated guide (if it keeps one) is updated in the
      same change.
- [ ] A ruling made along the way is written to `docs/decisions/` as its own
      file, so it is not re-litigated.
- [ ] `AGENTS.md` "Current state" reflects what this change actually leaves
      behind.
- [ ] **Every count this change's documentation states was compared against
      reality** - the number of checks, entries, panels, sources, rules. A
      stated count is the one kind of drift a reader cannot detect by reading.

---

## I. Wall and ledger

- [ ] The work item exists and its state matches reality.
- [ ] Events fired for the transitions that happened - a change with no trace
      is a change nobody can audit.
- [ ] No item claims an open pull request that is already merged or closed.
      *(Bookkeeping can outrun a merge; the next unit inherits the red.)*

---

## J. Pull request

- [ ] One pull request in the pipeline. Nothing else of yours is open or in CI.
- [ ] No push while checks are running.
- [ ] The green check is the one branch protection requires - the **name**
      matches. A reduced or draft-scoped run is not the full gate.
- [ ] Review threads answered by their owner only, each finding verified before
      being accepted or declined, with proof when declined.
- [ ] **The last review round has landed.** Nothing is in flight. Declaring
      done while a review is still running has been wrong every time it has
      been tried, and the round it discards was already paid for.
- [ ] The CI meter reading for this change is recorded in the report beside the
      result - what ran, and roughly what it cost. A run nobody can price is a
      run nobody can scope next time.
- [ ] The merge is the coordinator's call. Report green and stop.

---

## K. Where the change LANDS (published or deployed surfaces only)

Delete this section if nothing this repository ships leaves the repository.
Where the published output is the product - a site, a deployed tree, a served
artifact - a green release job proves only that the release script exited 0:

- [ ] **Verify on the serving side, after every release.** Fetch the
      published surfaces; compare served bytes to what the release pins;
      probe that private paths are unreachable. A check that reads the
      repository cannot see the site - an effect that leaves the system is
      not observable in the state it leaves behind, so assert where the
      effect lands. *(Measured: five new pages 404 for an hour while the
      deploy reported success; header-level protections that existed only in
      what the host sent, invisible to every repo-reading check.)*
- [ ] **Close-out conditions for such changes name the live check** -
      "merges AND the pages serve 200" - not the merge alone.
- [ ] **The live probe re-runs on a schedule**, because a host drifts
      without a commit: a panel change disables a header, a rewrite stops
      firing, and no repository event marks the day.

---

## Reporting back

State, explicitly:

1. What shipped, and the item key.
2. Which checks ran, with the commands and the counts.
3. Which checks were N/A and why.
4. What reviewed the change - and if nothing but you did, say that.
5. Anything found and filed outside the surface.
